Why picolibc's copies
---------------------

newlib holds the original two files, at newlib/libc/machine/m68k at commit
b097f95233d26da7af6887798297d1efadb75370. picolibc changed them: on the
68000 and 68010 its memcpy.S copies long words only when both pointers are
aligned to 4 bytes, where newlib's checks only that they are even; its
loops end on bpl, where newlib's mix bcc and bpl after dbra; it returns the
destination in a0 as well as d0; and it sets each function's size and
marks the stack as not executable. Both projects' files need m68kasm.h,
which in newlib carries no notice, so libterae writes its own.


What was looked at
------------------

memmove and memcmp come from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
port_memmove.c and port_memcmp.c include. The candidates found:

memmove  NetBSD's common/lib/libc/arch/m68k/string/bcopy.S, which its
         memmove.S includes (BSD-2-Clause and BSD-3-Clause), is wrong
         when built for ColdFire: its byte loops count down d0 while the
         count is in d1. mintlib's bcopy.S carries no licence. Linux's
         is C under the GPL.

memcmp   NetBSD's memcmp.S (BSD-3-Clause) is wrong when built for
         ColdFire: the test for a zero length follows the push of d2 and
         so examines d2, and the restore at the end pops into sp, not
         d2.


What is checked
---------------

Homebrew's clang has no M68k backend and zig's assembles nothing for it,
but clang preprocesses for m68k. The check runs port_memcpy.S and
port_memset.S through clang's preprocessor, assembles them with GNU as from
Homebrew's m68k-elf-binutils (2.47) for the 68000, 68020 and 68060 and for
ColdFire's MCF5206, MCF5208 and MCF5475, and checks that they define only
the names C reserves and leave nothing undefined. clang knows no ColdFire,
so it is given __mcoldfire__, the macro GCC defines for one. GNU as refuses
an instruction the processor lacks: the 68000 build of memcpy.S, with its
dbra, fails for ColdFire.

That check found a fault in libterae's m68kasm.h, now fixed: where the
compiler doesn't define __REGISTER_PREFIX__, as clang doesn't, it left the
registers without the % that GNU as needs on ELF.

port_memmove.c and port_memcmp.c are not compiled here, as nothing here
compiles C for m68k. Nothing in this folder has run: before it is relied
on, it needs a build with m68k-elf-gcc and a run on hardware or an emulator,
such as QEMU's mcf5208evb.
